11.0.0 — the top bar rebuilt: one block for Play, Record and Loop, one display for position and tempo, the rows on one grid

What is new since 10074. Each line is code that the Echoel Studio run builds; nothing here is
device-verified yet.
- Top bar: Play, Record and Loop sit in one block with a fine line between them, and the
  position, the tempo and Tap in a second block beside it, read as one display. In the same row
  or the row above sit the Echoelmusic mark, the song and its menu, the pulse while a pulse source
  is on, and Undo and Redo, with More (…) wherever the switches are not in the bar. Undo, Redo and
  More are plain symbols without a frame; with Button Shapes on (Settings › Accessibility ›
  Display & Text Size) they get one.
- Top bar on an iPhone in portrait: two rows, now also on a 375-point iPhone (SE, 12 mini, 13
  mini, XS, 11 Pro), at the default text size and Display Zoom. The mark's waves start above
  Play's left edge, More is centred above Tap, and the display reaches the right edge: the
  position takes the room the row leaves. Position and tempo use one size of numbers and stand
  on one line.
- Top bar: colour only shows a state. A switch that is on shows a light tile inside its key, and
  Write a red one, because it writes into the song; on an iPhone, More shows the light tile while
  MIDI Learn is on and the red one while Write Automation is on. Record's symbol is red before it
  records, the key is red while a take records, and the position turns red. While the song plays,
  Play becomes Stop and its square is green; when audio has to be restarted, the key shows an
  amber circular arrow (VoiceOver: Restart Audio). While a MIDI clock, a Jam or
  Flow sets the tempo, a lock stands before EXT, JAM or FLOW.
- Top bar: every number keeps one width, and the song's name is measured at one width, so a
  moving playhead, a new tempo, a changing pulse reading or a long song name never moves the bar
  into another layout; a long name is cut short. Switching a pulse source on or off can: where
  room is tight the bar changes layout (an iPhone SE in landscape then has two rows with the
  switches in sight).
- Top bar, the layouts: the bar follows the size of its window, not the kind of device. Where it
  is wide (an iPad at full screen) the switches (Click, MIDI, Keys, Field Window, Write) are a
  third block and the tempo has − and +; narrower, the switches stay in sight without − and +
  (an iPad mini in portrait); narrower still, the phone's two rows with the switches in More; in
  a narrow Slide Over window, three rows. On an iPhone in landscape the bar is one row with the switches in More.
  All of this is at the default text size and Display Zoom; larger text moves the bar to a
  layout with more rows sooner.
- The status lines under the bar (count-in, Loop Record, the Jam, MIDI Learn, errors, an export's
  loudness) start at its left edge instead of in the middle.

Known limit, unchanged since 10074: a MIDI take does not record a key held across the loop's end
as it was heard. While the record line shows "Loop Record · take n" (a loop of two bars or
longer), the note ends at the loop's end and the next take does not have it. On a one-bar loop, a
key let go after the loop came round is recorded shorter than it was held.

Previous: 11.0.0 (10074) — a key held through the loop sounds on, other makers' Audio Units in the
lists, a Samples folder to hear sounds from; 10073 — touch loudness, slice pads, the chords of the
song's key. Earlier: git history.

Bump this file on a commit whose "Echoel Studio" run is green to upload a build
(.github/workflows/studio-testflight.yml). Build number = 10000 + the workflow's run number.
The version is MARKETING_VERSION in EchoelStudio/project.yml.
This file is a test list for the device, not a source for claims (ContentPipeline/CLAIMS.md).

Five things to try on the device (answer each yes or no):
1. iPhone in portrait, text size and Display Zoom at their defaults: is the top bar two calm rows,
   with the tempo in the second, the mark's waves starting above Play and More (…) centred above
   Tap? Does it look orderly to you?
2. On an iPhone in portrait, tap the tempo, type a new one and press OK; do it again with another
   number, then tap Undo once, so neither Undo nor Redo is dimmed (Loop and the switches are no
   undo steps). Do Undo, Redo and More, the plain symbols at the top right, read as buttons, and
   are they easy to hit without touching a neighbour?
3. Loop on, then Play, then Record: is the lit Loop clear without outshining Play, is Record's
   symbol red before you press it, and while recording are the Record key and the position red?
4. The tempo: drag it up and down, tap it to type one, and tap Tap in time. Does each work as
   before? Then, with the song stopped, tap Arrange's ruler near bar 3 and tap the position: does
   it go back to bar 1?
5. iPad, or an iPhone turned to landscape: is every control in the bar or in More, and does
   nothing overlap? (Expected: the tempo has − and + only where the bar is wide, such as an iPad
   at full screen; elsewhere drag it or tap to type. The count-in is under Click: touch and hold
   it, or find it in More. A long song name ending in … is cut on purpose.)
